      "url": "https://www.songfacts.com/facts/puff-daddy/ill-be-missing-you",
      "title": "I'll Be Missing You by Puff Daddy - Songfacts",
      "retrievedAt": "[time]",
      "excerpt": "On July 1, 2007, Diddy performed \"I'll Be Missing You\" at the Concert for Diana held at Wembley Stadium in London. This performance was downloaded so many times that it returned the song to the UK chart, this time making #32. In America, this was the #1 song for most of the summer of 1997, claiming the top spot on June 14 and spending 11 weeks there (\"Candle In The Wind '97\" stayed for 14 weeks, starting October 11). [...] Notorious B.I.G. was signed to Diddy's Bad Boy label, and after the shoo…",
      "recordRef": null
    },
    {
      "id": "tavily:https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NKMtZm2YuBE",
      "source": "tavily",
      "sourceTier": "web",
      "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NKMtZm2YuBE",
      "title": "Puff Daddy [feat. Faith Evans & 112] - I'll Be Missing You (Official Music Video)",
      "retrievedAt": "[time]",
      "excerpt": "In 1993, Sean “Puff Daddy” Combs, also known as Diddy, launched Bad Boy Records. Nobody could have imagined the impact the label would have on pop culture. As a subsidiary of the larger Bad Boy Entertainment, the label became best known for its signature sound of streetwise hip-hop soul and the ghetto fabulousness that radiated like precious jewels from its very name. Bad Boy was designed for those who aspired higher, but also wanted to party until the break of dawn. Puffy had seen life from [.…",
      "recordRef": null
    },
    {
      "id": "tavily:https://www.vibe.com/features/editorial/bad-boy-for-life-20-facts-about-puff-daddy-the-familys-no-way-out-523343",
      "source": "tavily",
      "sourceTier": "web",
      "url": "https://www.vibe.com/features/editorial/bad-boy-for-life-20-facts-about-puff-daddy-the-familys-no-way-out-523343",
      "title": "Puff Daddy's No Way Out: 20 Facts About Sean Combs' Debut Album",
      "retrievedAt": "[time]",
      "excerpt": "At the height of the East coast versus West coast beef, Diddy decided to fly his camp of in-house producers, The Hitmen, out to Maraval, Trinidad, with the goal of creating hit records that would dominate radio for the foreseeable future. Camping out at the Caribbean Sound Basin studio, Diddy, The Hitmen, and a crew of engineers would craft the foundation of the sound that would take Bad Boy’s success and appeal to the stratosphere. [...] Diddy’s stable of producer’s, The Hitmen, would craft th…",
      "recordRef": null
    },
    {
      "id": "tavily:https://www.encyclopedia.com/african-american-focus/news-wires-white-papers-and-books/combs-sean",
      "source": "tavily",
      "sourceTier": "web",
      "url": "https://www.encyclopedia.com/african-american-focus/news-wires-white-papers-and-books/combs-sean",
      "title": "Combs, Sean | Encyclopedia.com",
      "retrievedAt": "[time]",
      "excerpt": "Also in 1997, Combs debuted as a recording artist with two singles: \"Can't Nobody Hold Me Down\" and \"I'll Be Missing You\" as well as an album, No Way Out. The multi-platinum \"I'll Be Missing You\" was the more successful single. Combs produced it as a tribute to Smalls, and it featured Combs, Faith Evans, and 112. When the song was performed at the 1997 MTV Awards, Combs became more widely known. No Way Out sold more than 561,000 copies in its first week and went on to sell eight million copies.",
      "recordRef": null
